A||N||9||Zorba||Turn Signal & Wiper||||8||
Z||000000||Zorba||08-19-2000||02:30 AM||||Something funny happened today. I turn the right turn signal and the wiper goes on too.<BR>I have had this happen in the past with another car when the battery wasn't fully charged. Has anyone had this problem? What causes the turn signal to activate the wiper too?||||reg||8
Z||000001||fropuff||08-19-2000||10:43 AM||||Mine does that too.  It doesn't turn the wiper all the way on, just swipes once.  The "mist" wiper setting on mine is very sensitive.  You don't have to turn it much to activate it.  So, when I activate the left turn signal, the "mist" setting goes into action. ||||reg||10
Z||000002||Zorba||08-19-2000||11:39 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by fropuff:<BR>[B] <P>SO how do we fix this annoying problem?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bruce||08-19-2000||12:08 PM||||You probably have to get a new wiper/turn indicator assembly. They're available thru the Fiero Store. Better yet, try your local dealership.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fropuff||08-19-2000||12:50 PM||||I'm just going to leave mine.  Doesn't hurt much.  It did scratch it when I had my license plate held by the wipers and they turned on. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||08-19-2000||03:27 PM||||for what it's worth, Rodney Dickman has replacement turnsignal/wiper lever assembly also........||||reg||1
Z||000006||btoth||08-20-2000||12:10 PM||||it seems to be a common GM problem in their 80's cars.  mine does it and i just hooked a switch to the fuse for the windsheild wipers so that when i hit the turn signal they won't go on if i don't need them.  it's still annoying.  i bought a new turn signal arm... didn't fix it... i've heard that you have to replace the part inside the steering column that the arm attaches to (?)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Zorba||08-20-2000||04:24 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by btoth:<BR>[B]it seems to be a common GM problem in their 80's cars.  mine does it and i just hooked a switch to the fuse for the windsheild wipers so that when i hit the turn signal  <P>Tell us more about your bypassing method. I am interested in that. A small switch hooked up to the fuse? That sounds better than messing with the steering column.||||reg||1
Z||000008||btoth||08-20-2000||06:00 PM||||all i did was buy a switch (i went with a push button one myself...) at radio shack.  attach it to a wire and and at the other ends of the wire attach two male connectors that can fit into the little slots that the fuse usually plugs into (remove the fuse first).  idealy you'd probably want to wire in the fuse with some sort of inline connector between the fuse box and the switch but i didn't really care at the time. hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||08-21-2000||03:25 PM||||It's called a "phantom wipe" problem. It's almost always the motor assembly. I believe a TSB came out for it across the GM product line back in the mid 80's.<P>Most noticeable when you first start the car. The wipers will cycle once. I replaced my motor and the problem was solved.<P>I imagine that someone that really cared could disassemble the motor and see what is happening. Likely a park problem.<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
